      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town began as Hillman Company timberland, platted in 1913 as Cathcart Division #1 after Isaac Cathcart’s forest was logged.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clearview%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hillman offered right‑of‑way to the Snohomish Fruit Growers Association, sparking a shorter route to Seattle that finished in 1925.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clearview%2C_Washington",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A church was built on land donated by Hillman, and another on property given by George Robinson, anchoring the community’s early faith life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clearview%2C_Washington",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Clearview’s name, likely coined by merchants DesMarais, reflects the sweeping Cascade Mountain vistas residents could enjoy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clearview%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town’s elevation on Clearview Hill gives drivers a clear view of the Cascades, a feature that helped shape its identity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
